10209: # (wave 28b, D2 / F36-2) The sender's lock <spool>/.flush.lock: a MARKER file {pid, start_time,
10210: # token, since}. Taken when it can be created (CreateNew). An existing one is refused while somebody
10211: # holds it open; otherwise it is TAKEN OVER - rewritten under an exclusive handle, so two senders
10212: # never both take it - (wave 28c, D4 / F42-7, F43-5, F44-2) ONLY when its owner process (pid and start
10213: # time) is gone: a lock with a living owner is left alone however old it is and reported ("sender busy
10214: # since <t>"); an owner whose identity cannot be confirmed counts as living (Test-PidAlive). A lock
10215: # that names no owner and is not held open (its writer died between creating and writing it) has no
10216: # owner left: it is taken over. Released (deleted) in `finally` by its owner only (the token); every sender checks its
10217: # token again right before each spool rewrite and each send (Test-TelemetryFlushLockMine).
10218: # { Ok; Token; TookOver ('' or why the old lock was taken over); Why (why refused) }.
10219: function Enter-TelemetryFlushLock {
